            <ArticleTitle>Ultrafast Dischargeable LiMn2O4 Thin-Film Electrodes with Pseudocapacitive Properties for Microbatteries.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>5295-5301</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1021/acsami.6b15258</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>LiMn2O4 (LMO) thin films are deposited on Si-based substrates with Pt current collector via multi-layer pulsed-laser-deposition technique. The LMO thin films feature unique kinetics that yield outstanding electrochemical cycling performance in an aqueous environment. At extremely high current densities of up to 1880 μA cm-2 (≈ 348 C), a reversible capacity of 2.6 μAh cm-2 is reached. Furthermore, the electrochemical cycling remains very stable for over 3500 cycles with a remarkable capacity retention of 99.996% per cycle. We provide evidence of significant nondiffusion-controlled, pseudocapacitive-like storage contribution of the LMO electrode.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
